{"name":"Wellerman","hex":"#54606a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[],"entries":[{"title":"Wellerman","blocks":[{"text":"\"Soon May the Wellerman Come\", also known as \"Wellerman\" or \"The Wellerman\", is a folk song in ballad style first published in New Zealand in the 1970s. The \"wellermen\" were supply ships owned by the Weller brothers, three merchant traders in the 1800s who were amongst the earliest European settlers of the Otago region of New Zealand."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wellerman","wikidata":"Q104837613"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/%E6%AF%9B%E5%88%A9%E8%A7%92_Maori_Head_-_panoramio.jpg/500px-%E6%AF%9B%E5%88%A9%E8%A7%92_Maori_Head_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/%E6%AF%9B%E5%88%A9%E8%A7%92_Maori_Head_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-%E6%AF%9B%E5%88%A9%E8%A7%92_Maori_Head_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/%E6%AF%9B%E5%88%A9%E8%A7%92_Maori_Head_-_panoramio.jpg/500px-%E6%AF%9B%E5%88%A9%E8%A7%92_Maori_Head_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Weller's Rock or Te Umukuri,[6] one of two lookout points for whales (along with Taiaroa Head) used by whalers.[5] Aramoana, across Otago Harbour, is visible in the background.[7]It has been designated as a historic reserve for protection.[8]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E6%AF%9B%E5%88%A9%E8%A7%92_Maori_Head_-_panoramio.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Through_DNA_fingerprinting_that_southern_right_whales_are_now_migrating_once_again_from_sub-Antarctic_islands_to_their_ancestral_calving_grounds_on_the_mainland_of_New_Zealand.jpg/500px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_